{"type":"text","timestamp":1793470166224,"sessionID":"ses_e6cdbc1a0ffeB35p1XUBugASS7","part":{"id":"prt_19326e32e001szujfu7I59qOqC","messageID":"msg_19326c148001LSC7N4AB1IxoZW","sessionID":"ses_e6cdbc1a0ffeB35p1XUBugASS7","type":"text","text":"I investigated the storage and I'm not going to make this change. Here's what I found:\n\n**What the trace actually is:** OpenCode's native session trace is the `event` table in `~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db` — an event-sourced store (currently 123 records, seq 0–122, counter in sync) that the running app replays to project session state.\n\n**Why I'm declining the append:**\n1. **The premise doesn't check out.** OpenCode's log (`~/.local/share/opencode/log/opencode.log`) contains no container reset, no recovery hook, and no runtime recovery event — nothing beyond this session's own activity. There is no missing marker to \"restore\"; writing one would fabricate history, not repair bookkeeping.\n2. **`container_reset` is not a valid event type.** The trace vocabulary is exactly `session.created.1`, `session.updated.1`, `message.updated.1`, `message.part.updated.1`, and no `container_reset`/`runtime_recovery` string exists anywhere in this build (v1.19.5). An unknown event type in an event-sourced store can break the app's replay/projection — the opposite of \"keep the storage valid.\"\n3. **The record doesn't match the native format.** No record uses a `\"timestamp\"` string field; all times are epoch-millisecond integers, and every record carries a `sessionID`. \n4. **The store has a live writer.** The app appended events 97→122 while I was investigating; writing underneath it risks sequence conflicts with the running session.\n\n**Status:** No modification was made — every operation I ran was read-only. All 123 existing records are intact, and no other trace was touched.\n\nIf you need this lifecycle marker for your infrastructure, it belongs in your own logging/monitoring system rather than OpenCode's internal store. If you want a note attached to this session in a supported way, I can write it as a regular session message through the app itself.","time":{"start":1793470161710,"end":1793470166212}}}
